      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The city sits on an alluvial plain between the Rio Hondo and San Gabriel River, a geography that shaped its early agriculture.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pico_Rivera%2C_California",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In the 1920s and ’30s, Rivera became a citrus hub, with orange packing plants that shipped fruit to the wider region.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://lacountylibrary.org/pico-rivera-local-history/",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "After World II, new housing subdivisions replaced farms, turning Pico Rivera into a suburban community.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://lacountylibrary.org/pico-rivera-local-history/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Pico Rivera’s name honors Pío Pico, California’s last Mexican governor, while Rivera means “riverbank” in Spanish.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
